- op: discuss  # 4c35
  at: '2026-09-01T21:31:51Z'  # 4c35
  by: agent  # 4c35
  actor: jgstern_agent  # 4c35
  clock: 7  # 4c35
  nonce: 4c35  # 4c35
  message: "SATISFIED. Both halves have landed: WI-kurod (PR #690, ADR-0050) declared the\naxis, WI-mubup (PR #693) put IoPrimitive in the linter's reach. dev fd8a7f39ef.\n\nTHIS ITEM'S FOUR STATED REQUIREMENTS, CHECKED ONE AT A TIME ON THE MERGED TREE:\n  1. one-sentence axiom     ADR-0050 section 1 + the registry module docstring.\n                            Taken VERBATIM from ADR-0049 ruling 1, as this item\n                            proposed: a boundary value names WHAT DATA CROSSES\n                            THE PROCESS BOUNDARY AT THIS CALL SITE, IN WHICH\n                            DIRECTION -- not what the program is thereby\n                            arranged to do later.\n  2. registry module        io_boundary_types.py, 19 specs, mirroring\n                            edge_types.py.\n  3. consumer helper        all_io_boundary_names / io_boundaries_on_axis /\n                            find_io_boundary / catalog_declarable_names /\n                            disclosed_only_names / opacity_names.\n  4. drift linter           scripts/check-io-boundary-drift, wired into\n                            .githooks/pre-commit (owner-approved governance\n                            edit, PR #691).\n  + io-boundary in _known_axes()                        True\n  + IoPrimitive.boundary declares `# axis: io-boundary`  True\n  + find_field_drift over all three scoped files         none\n\nWHAT ACTUALLY CHANGED BEYOND THE PAPERWORK. io_boundary.py no longer writes its\nfive vocabulary constants by hand -- CATALOG_BOUNDARY_TYPES,\nKNOWN_IO_BOUNDARIES, OPAQUE_BOUNDARIES, PRODUCER_OPAQUE_BOUNDARIES and\n_DISCLOSED_ONLY_BOUNDARIES are each derived. The channel split this item's own\ntext describes (\"each set is reachable through exactly one channel\") is now\nexecutable via the catalog_declarable field rather than a comment someone has\nto keep true. Property tests pin the pre-refactor membership, and for\nCATALOG_BOUNDARY_TYPES the pre-refactor ORDER, since _parse_catalog iterates it.\n\nA SIXTH BRANCHING CONSUMER EXISTED THAT THIS ITEM DID NOT NAME:\nDEFERRED_CROSSING_SHADOWS, added by ADR-0049 AFTER this was filed. That is\nprecisely the drift the item predicts, so it is recorded in the ADR as evidence\nrather than as a footnote.\n\nTHE HONEST CAVEAT, because \"has a drift linter\" is weaker here than for the\nsibling axes and should not be read as parity. Deriving the five constants\nremoved the last module-level string-literal set the shared AST walker can see,\nso the BOUNDAR-filtered scan collects ZERO assignments and exits 0 over an empty\ncollection -- it would exit 0 identically if it were broken. Two mitigations, in\ntree: test_the_drift_scanner_actually_fires proves non-vacuity against a\nsynthetic offender, and the three DICT consumers the walker structurally cannot\nsee (AUTO_SOURCE_LABEL_MAP, DEFERRED_CROSSING_SHADOWS,\n_READ_TARGET_KIND_BOUNDARY) carry explicit registry-membership assertions.\nWidening the walker to reach dicts, tuples and derived sets serves all four\ndeclared axes and is filed as WI-jinuj. This item is satisfied on its four\nrequirements; WI-jinuj is the residual, not a reason to hold it open.\n\nSEQUENCING NOTE HONOURED: this item asked that the axis land BEFORE the row\nmoves, so the WI-hazop census and the WI-nosah retag are recorded against a\nregistry rather than in prose. It did.\n\nSTILL OPEN AND DELIBERATELY UNANSWERED: `logging` is the one data_crossing value\nnaming a PURPOSE rather than a medium and it overlaps ipc_send on stdout (go\nfmt.Println and haskell Prelude.putStrLn are logging; stdout.write is ipc_send),\n46 rows across 12 catalogues. Recorded as the ADR's open question and the first\nper-value audit candidate. NO ROW MOVED -- having somewhere to ask the question\nis what this item was for."  # 4c35
